;; Kanata 1.12+
;; Framework Laptop US-ANSI QWERTY with mirrored home-row modifiers:
;;   A S D F = left  Meta Alt Ctrl Shift
;;   J K L ; = right Shift Ctrl Alt  Meta
;;
;; Home-row modifiers are for shortcuts only. Dedicated Shift types capitals,
;; so the home-row Shift never has to win a race against ordinary typing and is
;; tuned as defensively as every other home-row key.
;;
;; Typing always wins. A home-row key only becomes a modifier when nothing about
;; the keystroke looks like typing: no key pressed just before it, no same-hand
;; key pressed just after it, and either an opposite-hand key completed on top of
;; it or a deliberate hold past the timeout. Everything else types the letter.
;;
;; Multi-modifier shortcuts are not built by stacking undecided home-row keys on
;; top of each other. Exactly one home-row key makes a tap-hold decision; once it
;; resolves to a modifier it switches its own hand to the lmods/rmods layer,
;; where the remaining home-row keys are plain modifiers with no decision left to
;; make. There are two ways in:
;;
;;   - Roll onto the opposite hand: K then A is Ctrl+A, resolved the moment A is
;;     released. Add more modifiers from the K hand once inside the layer.
;;   - Hold one home-row key past the timeout: hold D, then F S A are literally
;;     Shift Alt Meta and any key on either hand is the target, same hand
;;     included. This is what Ctrl+click and Ctrl+Space need.
;;
;; Ctrl+Shift is the most used pair, so it also gets a chord that skips the wait:
;; D+F or J+K pressed together is Ctrl+Shift and opens that hand's layer at once,
;; which makes a four-modifier shortcut a chord plus two taps.
;;
;; Meta is on A and G, mirrored on ; and H, so both a pinky and an index-finger
;; Meta are available to compare. Holding one never sends a lone Super to the
;; desktop; see hold-lmet below.

(defcfg
  process-unmapped-keys yes
  concurrent-tap-hold yes

  ;; The primary defense. A home-row key pressed within this window of any other
  ;; key is its letter, immediately, with no waiting state and no decision to
  ;; get wrong. This covers every home-row key inside a burst of typing; only a
  ;; key that starts a burst is ever a modifier candidate at all.
  tap-hold-require-prior-idle 200

  ;; The same idea applied to chords: after any non-chord keypress, chord
  ;; matching is skipped entirely for this long. Mid-typing, D+F and F+J are
  ;; never buffered and never delay a keystroke; the chords only come alive at
  ;; the start of a burst, which is the only time a shortcut can start.
  chords-v2-min-idle 200
)

;; Caps Lock and Escape are swapped here rather than through an xkb option.
;; Doing it downstream of Kanata swapped Kanata's own Escape output back into
;; Caps Lock, which is what the F+J chord below was really emitting.
;;
;; Physical handedness. The hardware Fn key is handled by the keyboard firmware
;; and does not emit a normal keycode, so it is not part of this map. Tab, Enter,
;; Backspace, Escape, Caps Lock, and Delete are placed on the hand that actually
;; reaches them so that Shift+Tab, Ctrl+Backspace and friends resolve as holds.
;; Space is deliberately unassigned: it is the one key common enough right after
;; a home-row letter that treating it as a hold trigger would misfire. Dedicated
;; modifiers are excluded via hrm-neutral below rather than by handedness.
(defhands
  (left
    esc f1 f2 f3 f4 f5 f6
    grv 1 2 3 4 5
    tab q w e r t
    caps a s d f g
    z x c v b)

  (right
    f7 f8 f9 f10 f11 f12 del
    6 7 8 9 0 - = bspc
    y u i o p [ ] \
    h j k l ; ' ret
    n m , . /
    up left down rght))

(defvar
  ;; Hold this long with nothing else pressed to enter the modifier layer.
  ;; Short enough to be usable for Ctrl+click, long enough that it is only ever
  ;; reached by dwelling on a key deliberately: any same-hand key, any completed
  ;; opposite-hand key, and any preceding keystroke all resolve the decision
  ;; well before it expires.
  hrm-hold 300
  tap-repress 160
  combo-timeout 45

  ;; Dedicated modifier keys must not settle a pending home-row modifier;
  ;; they are meant to stack with one. Reused by every HRM alias below.
  hrm-neutral (neutral-keys lsft rsft lctl rctl lmet lalt ralt)
)

;; A lone Super press-and-release opens the desktop launcher, which a home-row
;; Meta would trigger every time it was held and then released without a target.
;; f24 is kanata's conventional placeholder: a real keycode nothing binds. The
;; hold presses it alongside Meta, so the desktop sees a non-modifier key during
;; the hold and the release is never lone. It is let go 5ms later rather than
;; held, so it cannot auto-repeat underneath the modifier.
;;
;; Alt has the same lone-press behaviour in GTK and Qt, where it focuses the
;; menu bar. Add f24 and the release macro to hr-s and hr-l to cover that too.
(defvirtualkeys
  rel-f24 (release-key f24))

(defalias
  hold-lmet (multi lmet f24 (layer-while-held lmods) (macro 5 (on-press tap-vkey rel-f24)))
  hold-rmet (multi rmet f24 (layer-while-held rmods) (macro 5 (on-press tap-vkey rel-f24)))

  ;; tap-hold-opposite-hand-release waits for the opposite-hand target key to be
  ;; pressed AND released before committing to the modifier. This is what makes
  ;; the config safe for a rolling typist: in a roll the home-row key comes up
  ;; while the next key is still down, and a home-row key released first is
  ;; always its letter. Only dwelling on the home-row key past the other key's
  ;; release produces a modifier.
  ;;
  ;; same-hand tap is the setting that matters most. A same-hand key press ends
  ;; the decision immediately as a letter, so "sad", "flag", "ask" and every
  ;; other same-hand roll emits without ever entering a waiting state. Stacking
  ;; same-hand modifiers is not this action's job; the lmods/rmods layers below
  ;; do that, and they do it without a pending decision to stall the key queue.
  ;;
  ;; neutral ignore lets a dedicated modifier stack with a pending HRM instead
  ;; of collapsing it to a tap. unknown-hand tap keeps Space tap-biased; reach
  ;; Ctrl+Space through the hold-then-layer path. timeout hold is the layer
  ;; entry: a deliberate hold with nothing else pressed is a modifier. Hold a
  ;; home-row key to repeat its letter by tapping it first, which tap-repress
  ;; turns into an ordinary auto-repeat.

  hr-a (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold a @hold-lmet
         (same-hand tap)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-s (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold s (multi lalt (layer-while-held lmods))
         (same-hand tap)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-d (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold d (multi lctl (layer-while-held lmods))
         (same-hand tap)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-f (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold f (multi lsft (layer-while-held lmods))
         (same-hand tap)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-g (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold g @hold-lmet
         (same-hand tap)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-h (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold h @hold-rmet
         (same-hand tap)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-j (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold j (multi rsft (layer-while-held rmods))
         (same-hand tap)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-k (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold k (multi rctl (layer-while-held rmods))
         (same-hand tap)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-l (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold l (multi ralt (layer-while-held rmods))
         (same-hand tap)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-; (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold ; @hold-rmet
         (same-hand tap)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  ;; Chord targets. Ctrl+Shift both presses the pair and opens the hand's layer,
  ;; so the other two modifiers are one tap away each.
  cs-l (multi lctl lsft (layer-while-held lmods))
  cs-r (multi rctl rsft (layer-while-held rmods))
)

;; D+F and J+K are Ctrl+Shift. F+J is Escape. None of these three pairs is an
;; English bigram, and chords-v2-min-idle keeps them from being consulted at all
;; while typing is in progress, so they cost ordinary typing nothing.
;;
;; These are disabled inside lmods and rmods: there the same keys are already
;; plain modifiers, and a chord would fight them.
(defchordsv2
  (d f) @cs-l $combo-timeout all-released (lmods rmods)
  (j k) @cs-r $combo-timeout all-released (lmods rmods)
  (f j) esc   $combo-timeout first-release (lmods rmods))

;; Framework Laptop US keyboard. The Fn key is not visible to Kanata.
(defsrc
  esc  f1   f2   f3   f4   f5   f6   f7   f8   f9   f10  f11  f12  del

  grv  1    2    3    4    5    6    7    8    9    0    -    =    bspc

  tab  q    w    e    r    t    y    u    i    o    p    [    ]    \

  caps a    s    d    f    g    h    j    k    l    ;    '    ret

  lsft z    x    c    v    b    n    m    ,    .    /    rsft

  lctl lmet lalt            spc            ralt rctl
                                      left up down rght
)

;; G and H are a second Meta alongside A and ;, kept so both positions can be
;; compared in use. They are the frequency risk in this layer: H especially is
;; common enough that if stray Metas show up while typing, these two are the
;; first thing to drop back to plain letters. They stay Meta in the layers below
;; either way, so dropping them here costs no shortcut.
(deflayer base
  caps f1    f2    f3    f4    f5   f6   f7    f8    f9    f10   f11   f12  del

  grv  1     2     3     4     5    6    7     8     9     0     -     =     bspc

  tab  q     w     e     r     t    y    u     i     o     p     [     ]     \

  esc  @hr-a @hr-s @hr-d @hr-f @hr-g @hr-h @hr-j @hr-k @hr-l @hr-; '     ret

  lsft z     x     c     v     b    n    m     ,     .     /     rsft

  lctl lmet  lalt              spc              ralt rctl
                                          left up down rght
)

;; Once a home-row modifier on one hand has resolved, that whole hand becomes
;; plain modifiers: no tap-hold decision, no timing, no way to get it wrong.
;; The opposite hand's home row is pinned back to plain letters at the same
;; time, so a target key there is instant and repeats normally instead of
;; running its own tap-hold decision underneath a held modifier.
;;
;; That pinning is why all four modifiers live on each hand: a shortcut takes
;; every modifier from one hand and its target from anywhere, rather than
;; splitting modifiers across both. Every other key stays transparent, so
;; same-hand targets like Ctrl+C work once the layer is open.
;;
;; These must stay below the base layer: Kanata treats the first layer in the
;; file as the default one.
(deflayermap (lmods)
  a lmet  s lalt  d lctl  f lsft  g lmet
  h h     j j     k k     l l     ; ;)

(deflayermap (rmods)
  h rmet  j rsft  k rctl  l ralt  ; rmet
  a a     s s     d d     f f     g g)
